%U A308088 6807306516856330830869430272,114384746962912858679314227396608
%N A308088 Number of normal matrices of order n over GF(2).
%C A308088 A matrix A is normal if A^T A = A A^T, where for this sequence multiplication is performed in the field of two elements.
%C A308088 Put S = A + A^T over GF(2). Then A is normal if and only if AS = SA. Necessarily, S is symmetric with zero diagonal and even row sums. For each such S, the conditions A + A^T = S and AS = SA form an affine linear system over GF(2). Summing its numbers of solutions counts all normal matrices. The computation can be accelerated by grouping S into canonical types using the classification of alternating-Hamiltonian operators. - _Vladeta Jovovic_, Oct 19 2026
%H A308088 Vladeta Jovovic, <a href="/A308088/b308088.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..80</a>
%H A308088 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/NormalMatrix.html">Normal Matrix</a>
%H A308088 <a href="/index/Mat#binmat">Index entries for sequences related to binary matrices</a>
%F A308088 Let s_m = 2^(m^2)*Product_{j=1..m} (4^j-1), G(x) = exp(Sum_{k>=1} (x^k/k)*Sum_{d|k} d*8^(k/d)/(4^(k/d)-1)), and C(x) = G(x)/Product_{j>=1} (1-4*x^j). Then a(2*m+1) = 2*s_m*[x^m]C(x) and a(2*m+2) = 2*4^m*s_m*[x^(m+1)](C(x)-G(x)) for m >= 0. - _Vladeta Jovovic_, Oct 19 2026
%Y A308088 A055547 counts 0-1 matrices normal over the integers.
